<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Communist Manifesto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Communist Manifesto, Manifesto of the Communist Party</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Communist Manifesto, originally the Manifesto of the Communist Party, is a political pamphlet by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a>, commissioned by the Communist League and published anonymously in German in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in February 1848. It argues that &quot;the history of all hitherto existing society is the history of class struggles&quot; and predicts that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a> will overthrow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a> and bring about a classless society. It fell into obscurity after the defeat of the revolutions of 1848, revived in the 1870s and has become one of the world's most influential political documents.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto.jpg" alt="The Communist Manifesto" width="320" height="501" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Friedrich Engels, Karl Marx · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Communist-manifesto.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In spring 1847 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> joined the League of the Just, which became the League of Communists. At its first congress, held from 2 to 9 June, the League asked Engels to draft a statement of its programme. He wrote the Draft of a Communist Confession of Faith, a catechism of 22 questions, and in October produced the Principles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">Communism</a>, a longer one of 25 questions. Several scholars see the kernel of the Manifesto in this second text.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> delayed the work while lecturing on political economy and writing for the Deutsche-Brüsseler-Zeitung. In late January 1848 the League's Central Committee sent him an ultimatum demanding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> by 1 February. The Manifesto was written over six to seven weeks, and only one page of draft is known to survive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a> is credited as co-author, but the final text was penned by Marx; Engels wrote in 1883 that its basic thought belonged solely to Marx.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Manifesto has a preamble and four sections. The preamble opens with the line about a spectre haunting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and urges communists to state their views openly. The first section, Bourgeois and Proletarians, sets out historical materialism: all earlier societies were marked by an exploiting minority and an exploited majority, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> the industrial working class, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a>, stands against the owners of the means of production, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a>.</p><p>The authors argue that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a>, by constantly revolutionising production, displaced feudal powers, and that in doing so it creates its own grave-diggers. In their view the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a> will become conscious of its power and overthrow it, leading to a classless society. They also proposed ten measures for a society moving towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, which their 1872 preface called dated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The pamphlet was published anonymously in late February 1848 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, as a 23-page German text issued by the Communist Workers' Educational Association. Marx was expelled by the Belgian police on 4 March. The first English version, translated by Helen Macfarlane, appeared in November 1850 in George Julian Harney's Chartist paper The Red Republican, which also named the authors for the first time.</p><p>After years of obscurity the text revived in the early 1870s, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a>'s role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association">First International</a>, his support for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a> of 1871 and the treason trial of German Social Democratic leaders, where prosecutors read it into the record. A new German edition of 1872 first used the short title The Communist Manifesto. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> of 1917 it became required reading for Leninist parties, and by its centenary in 1948 general publishers were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> it widely.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Communist Manifesto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels, whose <b>Communist Manifesto</b> appeared in 1848, argued that the working class must take power and end the private ownership of the means of production.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Industrial Revolution advanced, socialist critics blamed capitalism for the misery of the proletariat of factory workers, and in 1848 Marx and Engels offered a new definition of communism in <b>The Communist Manifesto</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Marx and Engels wrote <b>The Communist Manifesto</b> in 1847–48, just before the Revolutions of 1848, expressing what they termed scientific socialism, and by the late 19th century socialism had come to mean anti-capitalism and a post-capitalist system based on some form of social ownership.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Lennon called it &quot;anti-religious, anti-nationalistic, anti-conventional, anti-capitalistic&quot;, but accepted because it was &quot;sugarcoated&quot;, and said it was close to <b>The Communist Manifesto</b> although he was not a Communist; Ono described it as what Lennon believed, that all are one country, one world, one people.</blockquote><a href="/songs/imagine">Imagine</a></li></ul></section>
